  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1155 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nWeekend weather is looking a little more interesting now that\norganization of a tropical system is underway in the southwest\nGulf. Medium-range guidance has remained fairly consistent with\nthis system progressing north between a subtropical ridge over the\nBahamas and an upper-level trough moving into the Southern Plains\nFriday into Saturday. This should provide a favorable trajectory \ninto the Tennessee Valley on Saturday. As such, presence of this \nresidual tropical cyclone and anomalous moisture has led to \nfurther increases in rain chances/amounts this weekend. Generally\nspeaking, rain amounts of 1-2\" are forecast across Middle \nTennessee, with a low chance (<30%) of amounts 3\" or more. \nHeaviest rainfall is currently expected Saturday evening through \nSunday morning. Thankfully the rather progressive nature of the \nsystem is keeping rainfall amounts in check. This is due to the \ntropical low merging with the upper-level trough moving out of the\nSouthern Plains. Thus, at this time, we don't expect significant \nimpacts to Middle Tennessee - just beneficial rainfall and perhaps\nsome breezy winds. Nonetheless, we'll monitor forecast changes \nover the next couple of days closely. We should transition back to\ndrier, warmer weather early next week after this system departs \nto the northeast.",
